WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:06.291
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level five.

2
00:00:06.591 --> 00:00:10.116
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:10.416 --> 00:00:15.611
Unit thirteen: Legal Writing. Today you will state requirements with the mandative subjunctive.

4
00:00:15.911 --> 00:00:18.447
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:22.598
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.450 --> 00:00:24.672
Legal brief.

7
00:00:25.683 --> 00:00:29.761
Legal brief means a written argument given to a court to support one side.

8
00:00:30.350 --> 00:00:32.837
The lawyer filed a legal brief on Monday.

9
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:36.276
Your turn. Say: Legal brief.

10
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:40.251
Legal brief.

11
00:00:41.017 --> 00:00:42.378
Case citation.

12
00:00:43.383 --> 00:00:47.380
Case citation means a reference that identifies a court decision.

13
00:00:47.983 --> 00:00:50.598
Each case citation gives the year and court.

14
00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:54.196
Your turn. Say: Case citation.

15
00:00:57.033 --> 00:00:58.429
Case citation.

16
00:00:59.183 --> 00:01:00.951
Statutory interpretation.

17
00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.656
Statutory interpretation means deciding what the words of a law mean.

18
00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.573
Statutory interpretation depends on exact wording.

19
00:01:10.183 --> 00:01:13.811
Your turn. Say: Statutory interpretation.

20
00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:18.620
Statutory interpretation.

21
00:01:19.383 --> 00:01:20.675
Legal argument.

22
00:01:21.683 --> 00:01:25.680
Legal argument means reasoning that applies law to the facts of a case.

23
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:29.188
A strong legal argument cites clear authority.

24
00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:32.772
Your turn. Say: Legal argument.

25
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:37.018
Legal argument.

26
00:01:37.783 --> 00:01:39.504
Precedent analysis.

27
00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:45.233
Precedent analysis means studying earlier court decisions to guide a new case.

28
00:01:45.817 --> 00:01:49.534
Precedent analysis showed the court had ruled the same way before.

29
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:53.464
Your turn. Say: Precedent analysis.

30
00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:57.893
Precedent analysis.

31
00:01:58.650 --> 00:01:59.895
Plain language.

32
00:02:00.883 --> 00:02:04.706
Plain language means clear wording that ordinary readers can understand.

33
00:02:05.317 --> 00:02:07.699
Plain language makes contracts fairer.

34
00:02:08.283 --> 00:02:11.563
Your turn. Say: Plain language.

35
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:15.703
Plain language.

36
00:02:16.450 --> 00:02:17.915
Legal memorandum.

37
00:02:18.917 --> 00:02:23.296
Legal memorandum means an internal document that explains the law on an issue.

38
00:02:23.883 --> 00:02:26.649
The legal memorandum summarised three cases.

39
00:02:27.250 --> 00:02:30.332
Your turn. Say: Legal memorandum.

40
00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:34.690
Legal memorandum.

41
00:02:35.450 --> 00:02:36.683
Persuasive brief.

42
00:02:37.683 --> 00:02:42.121
Persuasive brief means a brief written to convince a court to rule in one party's favour.

43
00:02:42.717 --> 00:02:45.297
A persuasive brief must still be accurate.

44
00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:48.640
Your turn. Say: Persuasive brief.

45
00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:52.942
Persuasive brief.

46
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:57.698
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:58.550 --> 00:03:05.057
Visal, a rule says 'The court requires that each party submit a written statement.' Why 'submit' and not 'submits'?

48
00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:18.940
It is the mandative subjunctive. After verbs of demand, such as require, insist, recommend or propose, use the base verb for all subjects. No -s, no tense.

49
00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:21.597
What about the verb be?

50
00:03:22.683 --> 00:03:34.835
It is essential that every claim be supported by a citation. The base form be also appears in the negative: It is vital that the document not be altered. Use not before the verb.

51
00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.845
Is there a simpler option?

52
00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:52.016
Yes. Many writers use should: The court requires that each party should submit… Plain language often prefers this, but the base form is more formal and common in legal drafting.

53
00:03:53.167 --> 00:03:54.934
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:55.783 --> 00:04:01.359
Grammar focus: Mandative subjunctive: require and insist that plus base verb.

55
00:04:01.659 --> 00:04:02.904
Look at the pattern.

56
00:04:03.617 --> 00:04:07.532
The court requires that each party submit a written statement.

57
00:04:08.417 --> 00:04:11.658
It is essential that every claim be supported by a citation.

58
00:04:12.550 --> 00:04:15.362
The judge insisted that the document not be altered.

59
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:24.862
After require, insist, recommend, propose or it is essential / vital that, use the base verb for every subject. Use not before the verb for the negative.

60
00:04:25.617 --> 00:04:29.070
Be careful! Don't say: She demanded that he leaves.

61
00:04:31.033 --> 00:04:33.207
Say: She demanded that he leave.

62
00:04:33.507 --> 00:04:35.948
Use the base verb after demand that.

63
00:04:36.733 --> 00:04:39.374
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:40.167 --> 00:04:41.528
Writing for the Court.

65
00:04:41.828 --> 00:04:46.962
A legal brief is a written argument that asks a court to rule in one party's favour.

66
00:04:47.967 --> 00:04:53.925
The court requires that every case citation be accurate and that each statute be quoted exactly.

67
00:04:54.933 --> 00:05:00.787
A lawyer recommends that the legal memorandum explain statutory interpretation in simple terms.

68
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:07.004
Precedent analysis is vital, because judges insist that similar cases be treated alike.

69
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.887
It is also desirable that plain language replace unnecessary jargon, so that ordinary readers can follow the argument.

70
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:20.037
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:20.883 --> 00:05:22.918
The judge asked that he blank the form.

72
00:05:23.218 --> 00:05:26.848
A, sign. B, signs. C, signed.

73
00:05:30.400 --> 00:05:32.678
The answer is A: sign.

74
00:05:33.817 --> 00:05:37.186
Deciding what the words of a law mean is statutory blank.

75
00:05:37.486 --> 00:05:41.592
A, interpretation. B, citation. C, memorandum.

76
00:05:45.133 --> 00:05:47.609
The answer is A: interpretation.

77
00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.436
Which sentence is correct?

78
00:05:50.736 --> 00:05:57.826
A, It is vital that she is present.. B, It is vital that she be present.. C, It is vital that she being present..

79
00:06:01.367 --> 00:06:04.318
The answer is B: It is vital that she be present..

80
00:06:05.483 --> 00:06:09.642
Studying earlier court decisions to guide a new case is blank analysis.

81
00:06:09.942 --> 00:06:13.642
A, plain. B, precedent. C, persuasive.

82
00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.292
The answer is B: precedent.

83
00:06:20.450 --> 00:06:23.936
According to the reading, what should replace unnecessary jargon?

84
00:06:24.236 --> 00:06:28.829
A, plain language. B, longer citations. C, case notes.

85
00:06:32.367 --> 00:06:34.982
The answer is A: plain language.

86
00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:39.260
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:40.117 --> 00:06:44.763
Why might plain language be more effective than complex legal jargon in some legal writing?

88
00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:54.981
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:55.281 --> 00:07:20.845
Plain language is often more effective because the readers of legal documents are not always lawyers. If a contract or notice is written in clear words, people can understand their rights and duties and make fair decisions. Complex jargon can hide meaning, cause mistakes and even keep people from using the law. Clear writing is also easier for judges to check.

90
00:07:21.145 --> 00:07:22.842
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:23.750 --> 00:07:30.268
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and mandative subjunctive: require and insist that plus base verb.

92
00:07:30.568 --> 00:07:36.019
Today's value is precision of language. Choose your words carefully so others understand you.

93
00:07:36.319 --> 00:07:39.145
See you in Unit fourteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:39.445 --> 00:07:42.202
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

